1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,560
Start with photographs of the tools you want to trace.

2
00:00:02,560 --> 00:00:07,098
Photograph tools on the provided templates to make scaling easier.

3
00:00:07,098 --> 00:00:14,137
Here are the photographs we took of the air duster, adapters, and angled nozzle.

4
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:22,179
Some of the shadowing isn’t ideal. We’ll correct it when editing the outlines.

5
00:00:22,179 --> 00:00:35,343
Start with a new project. We’ll build this bin from the tool outlines.

6
00:00:35,343 --> 00:00:42,015
Print the calibration sheet at 100% and check its 100 mm reference bar.

7
00:00:42,015 --> 00:00:47,539
Follow the physical edge of the tool. Shadows and reflections can distort the trace.

8
00:00:47,539 --> 00:00:52,389
Right-click to remove extra points. Keep the corners that define the real tool.

9
00:00:52,389 --> 00:01:07,488
Drag points to refine the shape, using the real tool and its measurements as your guide.

10
00:01:07,488 --> 00:01:14,236
Click an edge to add a needed corner, then drag the new point into position.

11
00:01:14,236 --> 00:01:25,955
Check reflective areas carefully. The pocket should follow the outside edge.

12
00:01:25,955 --> 00:01:32,909
Right-click to remove extra points. Keep the corners that define the real tool.

13
00:01:32,909 --> 00:01:53,798
Drag points to refine the shape, using the real tool and its measurements as your guide.

14
00:01:53,798 --> 00:02:17,390
Click an edge to add a needed corner, then drag the new point into position.

15
00:02:17,390 --> 00:02:28,400
Remove the false notch while preserving the nozzle’s real corners.

16
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:32,541
Right-click to remove extra points. Keep the corners that define the real tool.

17
00:02:32,541 --> 00:02:42,140
Drag points to refine the shape, using the real tool and its measurements as your guide.

18
00:02:42,140 --> 00:02:50,203
Click an edge to add a needed corner, then drag the new point into position.

19
00:02:50,203 --> 00:03:00,823
Set the bin footprint, then size the pockets before fine-tuning their positions.

20
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:09,694
Drag each pocket into a rough position. We’ll check the size and gaps next.

21
00:03:09,694 --> 00:03:23,014
Use position and rotation to arrange the tools with room around each pocket.

22
00:03:23,014 --> 00:03:36,200
Round the pocket edges. Adjust clearance after trying the printed fit check.

23
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:42,668
In addition to the photos, we’ll create a pocket for the USB cable using Finger access.

24
00:03:42,668 --> 00:04:00,141
Measure the coiled cable, then set the slot’s width, length, and depth. Calipers help with depth.

25
00:04:00,141 --> 00:04:15,200
Measure the real tool with calipers. Compare its length, handle width, and head width with the outline.

26
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:20,865
Keep proportions links width and length. Unlock it when the X and Y corrections differ.

27
00:04:20,865 --> 00:04:34,952
Use your measured dimensions to size the other tools. These values are for this example.

28
00:04:34,952 --> 00:04:42,352
Export a thin fit check now, before committing to the full bin.

29
00:04:42,352 --> 00:04:50,588
Tool outlines uses less material to check the three photographed tools.

30
00:04:50,588 --> 00:05:01,434
Choose Full surface to include the USB cable pocket created with Finger access.

31
00:05:01,434 --> 00:05:05,466
The fit-check files are saved. Print at 100% scale and try the real tools.

32
00:05:05,466 --> 00:05:10,524
Place the real tools in the printed fit check. Use the fit and your measurements to guide adjustments.

33
00:05:10,524 --> 00:05:14,729
If needed, adjust the contour, size, or clearance and print another fit check.

34
00:05:14,729 --> 00:05:21,427
A thin fit check tests the openings. Measure pocket depth and tool seating separately.

35
00:05:21,427 --> 00:05:31,926
Adjust pocket depth based on measurements of each real tool. Use calipers for accurate depth measurements.

36
00:05:31,926 --> 00:05:55,323
Add two more finger-access scoops so the tools are easy to lift out.

37
00:05:55,323 --> 00:06:09,478
Use a gray body with black pocket floors and a black rim.

38
00:06:09,478 --> 00:06:12,346
The black floor layer reaches the recesses between the base feet.

39
00:06:12,346 --> 00:06:15,264
Reduce pocket depth from 39 to 38.5 mm to leave more floor underneath.

40
00:06:15,264 --> 00:06:19,171
The warning clears. The tool sits 0.5 mm higher; check that seating depth.

41
00:06:19,171 --> 00:06:30,924
Inspect the rounded edges, pocket floors, cable pocket, and finger scoops.

42
00:06:30,924 --> 00:06:38,823
Cross-section View helps inspect the interior. The exported model remains whole.

43
00:06:38,823 --> 00:06:44,153
After the real tools fit and pocket depths are checked, export the full bin.

44
00:06:44,153 --> 00:06:49,739
Save the finished bin and an editable project backup.

45
00:06:49,739 --> 00:06:55,116
Keep the editable project with the print file. Check the slicer preview before printing.

46
00:06:55,116 --> 00:07:00,957
Use Project → Export project to save another editable backup later.

47
00:07:00,957 --> 00:07:05,337
Print the fit test first. Then check the full model in your slicer.
